      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To solve for \\( f(x) \\) such that \\( f(x) + 2f \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) = 3x \\), we start by substituting \\( \\frac{1}{x} \\) in the equation. This gives us\n\\[ f \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) + 2f(x) = \\frac{3}{x}. \\]\nWe now have a system of linear equations in terms of \\( f(x) \\) and \\( f \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) \\):\n\\[ f(x) + 2f \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) = 3x \\]\n\\[ 2f(x) + f \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) = \\frac{3}{x}. \\]\nTo eliminate \\( f \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) \\), we multiply the first equation by 2 and subtract the second equation:\n\\[ 2f(x) + 4f \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) = 6x \\]\n\\[ 2f(x) + f \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) = \\frac{3}{x}. \\]\nSubtracting the second from the first:\n\\[ 3f \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) = 6x - \\frac{3}{x}. \\]\nMultiplying both sides by \\( \\frac{1}{3} \\):\n\\[ f \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) = 2x - \\frac{1}{x^2}. \\]\nNow substitute \\( f \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) \\) back into the first original equation:\n\\[ f(x) + 2 \\left( 2x - \\frac{1}{x^2} \\right) = 3x. \\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[ f(x) + 4x - \\frac{2}{x^2} = 3x. \\]\nIsolating \\( f(x) \\):\n\\[ f(x) = 3x - 4x + \\frac{2}{x^2} = -x + \\frac{2}{x^2}. \\]\nNow, we need to find the real solutions to \\( f(x) = f(-x) \\). We set \\( f(x) \\) equal to \\( f(-x) \\):\n\\[ -x + \\frac{2}{x^2} = -(-x) + \\frac{2}{(-x)^2}, \\]\n\\[ -x + \\frac{2}{x^2} = x + \\frac{2}{x^2}. \\]\nSubtracting \\( \\frac{2}{x^2} \\) from both sides:\n\\[ -x = x, \\]\n\\[ 2x = 0, \\]\n\\[ x = 0. \\]\nSince \\( f(x) \\) is not defined for \\( x = 0 \\), there are no real solutions to \\( f(x) = f(-x) \\).\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[ \\boxed{\\text{No real solutions}} \\]",
